  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n===== Frost and Freeze Headlines Overnight into Tomorrow AM =====\n\nCold air advection in place today over the Ohio Valley has kept \ntemperatures mainly in the mid 50s with upper 50s to near 60 across \nthe south and low 50s in the Bluegrass due to stubborn lingering \ncloud cover. Sfc high pressure over the central US will continue to \nbuild over the Ohio Valley tonight into tomorrow. This will help to \nclear out any lingering cloud cover and result in light winds \novernight. Strong radiational cooling is still expected once skies \nclear and winds become light. We will update both the Frost Advisory \nand Freeze Warning with no changes at this time with the caveat that \na few counties along the current Freeze Warning may need to be \nadded. Some of the current hesitation was due to probabilities for \nlows at or below freezing tomorrow morning in the HREF and NBM had \ndecreased with model guidance suggesting some light winds may linger \novernight. Widespread morning lows in the mid to low 30s is still \nanticipated across central KY and southern IN. \n \n\n===== Dry & Seasonal Weather All of Next Week =====\n\nA fairly stagnant weather pattern will be in place through most of \nthe week before we see some changes by the end of the week and into \nnext week. Main flow of the jet stream will be fairly zonal along \nand just north of the US/Canada border. Two cut-off lows, one over \nthe Atlantic off the Mid-Atlantic coast and one in the Pacific off \nthe California coast will lock upper ridging over the central US. \nThis will allow for sfc high pressure to keep weather mainly dry \nalong with temperatures returning to near to slightly above mid to \nlate October normals with highs in the low/mid 70s.",
